downloading your Genome on ISAAC
-> go to NCBI Genome database and search you target organism's assembly
-> check your assembly to ensure it is of good quality and has no lacking assemblies
-> navigate to the top of the page and select "FTP" ; it will open up a new page with many file paths
-> in your terminal, or code editor, ssh into ISAAC datatransfer node dtn1 or dtn2
bash:> ssh <netID>@dtn1.isaac.utk.edu
-> cd or mkdir to your desired directory for the genome
-> from the FTP page, right click on the genome file, it will end in something like "genomic.fna.gz"
-> copy the https:// path from the menu and head back to the terminal
-> from your desired directory:
bash:> wgets <paste filepath>
-> depending on the size of your genome, this might take several minutes
-> the downloaded genome is currently zipped, so to view the data, you will have to use command:
bash:> catz <filename> | head -n 10

downloading SRA data on ISAAC
-> go to NCBI SRA database and search your target organism
-> at the top of the page select "Send to:" -> "File" -> format: "Full XML" -> "Create File"
-> open your xml file in excell, or similar, and choose your desired organisms
-> in your terminal, or code editor, ssh into ISAAC datatransfer node dtn1 or dtn2.isaac.utk.edu
bash:> ssh <netID>@dtn1.isaac.utk.edu
-> cd or mkdir to your desired directory for sra data
-> create a file "SraAccList.txt" and insert your desired SRA numbers from the XML file
bash:> module load sra-tools/3.4.1
bash:> prefetch --option-file SraAccList.txt
-> add flag " --max-size <amount>G " before the second flag in order to bypass thee built in 20G limit
